To modify the `highlightCurrentPage` function to also add the `active` class to the `.logo` element, you should consider when the `.logo` should be active. Typically, the logo serves as a link back to the homepage (e.g., `index.html`). Therefore, it makes sense to add the `active` class to the logo when the user is currently on the homepage.

Here's how you can modify the function:

```javascript
// Function to highlight the current page in the navbar
function highlightCurrentPage() {
  // Get the current page filename
  const currentPage = window.location.pathname.split('/').pop() || 'index.html'; // Default to index.html if no filename

  // Find all nav links
  const navLinks = document.querySelectorAll('.nav-links a');

  // Loop through links and add 'active' class to the current page
  navLinks.forEach(link => {
    const href = link.getAttribute('href');
    if (href === currentPage) {
      link.classList.add('active');

      // Add active class to SVG icons
      const svgIcons = link.querySelectorAll('.magnifying-glass, .home-icon');
      svgIcons.forEach(icon => {
        icon.classList.add('active-icon');
      });

      // If this is in a dropdown, also highlight parent
      const parentDropdown = link.closest('.dropdown');
      if (parentDropdown) {
        const parentLink = parentDropdown.querySelector('.dropdown-toggle');
        if (parentLink) {
          parentLink.classList.add('active-parent');
        }
      }
    }
  });

  // --- NEW MODIFICATION START ---

  // Get the .logo element
  const logo = document.querySelector('.logo');

  // If the logo element exists and the current page is the homepage (index.html),
  // add the 'active' class to it.
  // This assumes the .logo typically links to the homepage.
  if (logo) {
    if (currentPage === 'index.html') {
      logo.classList.add('active');
    }
  }

  // --- NEW MODIFICATION END ---
}
```

**Explanation of the change:**

1.  **`const logo = document.querySelector('.logo');`**: This line selects the first element in the document that has the class `logo`.
2.  **`if (logo)`**: This checks if the `.logo` element was found (it would be `null` if not found), preventing errors.
3.  **`if (currentPage === 'index.html')`**: This condition checks if the currently active page (derived from the URL) is `index.html`. This is a common convention for a homepage.
4.  **`logo.classList.add('active');`**: If both conditions are met (logo exists and we are on the homepage), the `active` class is added to the logo element, allowing it to be styled (e.g., highlighted) via CSS.

This approach ensures that the logo is highlighted contextually when it represents the "current page" (i.e., the homepage).

